-- Passage rg12-connections-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a2cbc400-e47b-5386-892a-8feb50abfc3f', 'rg12-connections-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Violet of Marrowford$q$, $q$In 1868, a young chemist named Teodor Halvik was trying to make a cheaper medicine from coal tar, a sticky waste left over when the gas works of the fictional city of Marrowford heated coal to light the streets. The experiment failed as medicine, but it left a deep purple residue in his flask that stained everything it touched. Halvik noticed that the stain survived repeated washing, and he realized that he had made something the cloth trade badly wanted: a strong color that would not fade.

Until then, purple cloth had been expensive because the only reliable purple dye came from a rare lichen gathered by hand on coastal cliffs. Halvik's dye could be made in a vat from a substance the gas works were paying to throw away. Within three years, he and a cloth merchant named Rosa Ebeling had opened a dye works on the Marrow River, and purple dresses, scarves and ribbons appeared in shop windows that had never stocked them before.

The success drew imitators. Other chemists began treating coal tar with different acids and heat, hoping to find colors of their own, and by 1880 Marrowford had eleven dye works producing reds, greens and blues as well as Halvik's violet. To compete, the factories hired university-trained chemists and built laboratories, and the city's small technical college doubled its chemistry classes to supply them.

The same factories, however, poured their spent dye baths straight into the river. Downstream farmers complained that the water had turned strange colors and that their cattle refused to drink it. In 1884 the city council, pressed by the farmers and by a physician named Wilhelmina Osei, who had traced a cluster of skin rashes to bathing in the river, required every dye works to settle and filter its waste before releasing it.

The rule had an effect that no one on the council predicted. To meet it, the factories' chemists had to study what was actually in their waste, and in doing so they found several compounds worth selling. One became a common antiseptic; another was used to develop photographs. By 1900 the laboratories that had been built to invent colors were earning as much from these byproducts as from dye. Halvik's failed medicine had, by a long and indirect road, become a source of real medicines after all.$q$, 'draft')
