-- Passage rg12-connections-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3e6cd1b1-322c-5a0c-b6d7-6f4d3820f9ca', 'rg12-connections-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-connections'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Dry Summer of Tallow Creek$q$, $q$The fictional town of Tallow Creek had never metered its water. Each household paid the same flat fee, whether it watered a large lawn or only filled a kettle, and for decades the reservoir in the hills held more than enough for everyone.

That changed in the summer of 2016, when a second year of low snowfall left the reservoir at a third of its usual level. The town's water manager, Gideon Achterberg, calculated that at the current rate of use the reservoir would run dry by early October. Asking residents to cut back helped for a week or two, but use crept up again, and Achterberg had no way to tell which neighborhoods were using the most.

The town council paid for meters on every home, a project that took most of the autumn. The meters were intended only to measure use, but the first month of readings revealed something else. A small number of homes were recording water use day and night, even when their owners were away. Achterberg's staff visited them and found leaking pipes beneath floors and yards, some of which had been running unnoticed for years. Repairing those leaks alone saved nearly a tenth of the town's daily use.

The meters also made a new pricing plan possible. In 2017 the council replaced the flat fee with a charge based on use, with a low rate for basic needs and a higher one above that. Lawn watering fell sharply, and several residents who had resented the meters at first began comparing their monthly numbers with their neighbors. A retired engineer, Marisol Quintero, organized a group that taught residents to read their own meters and check for leaks themselves.

When normal snowfall returned in 2018, the reservoir refilled, but use never climbed back to its old level. The drought had ended, yet the meters, the pricing plan and Quintero's group remained, and Tallow Creek now uses about a quarter less water than it did before the dry summer.$q$, 'draft')
