-- Passage rg12-context-clues-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9e2ee895-e308-57c5-b15f-9300a0d96898', 'rg12-context-clues-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Animal That Waits Out Disaster$q$, $q$Tardigrades are tiny animals, rarely longer than a grain of salt, that live in moss, soil, and the sediment at the bottom of lakes. Under a microscope they look like plump, eight-legged bears lumbering through a droplet of water. Their appearance is charming, but their reputation rests on something far stranger: they can survive conditions that would kill almost any other creature.

Consider a patch of moss on a sunbaked rooftop. For weeks at a time it may be thoroughly inhospitable, too dry and too hot to support the active life of nearly any organism. When the moss dries out, a tardigrade does not try to escape. Instead it pulls in its legs, sheds most of the water in its body, and curls into a compact shape that researchers call a tun. In this state the animal is desiccated: it has lost nearly all of its moisture, shriveling until it looks more like a speck of dust than a living thing.

A tun is not dead, however. It is quiescent, its metabolism slowed so dramatically that it can scarcely be measured, much like a seed waiting in a drawer for spring. Add water, and within hours the animal unfolds and resumes walking and feeding as though nothing had happened.

In the dried state, tardigrades appear almost impervious to harm. Laboratory tests have exposed tuns to extreme cold, intense radiation, and crushing pressure, and many have revived afterward with no apparent damage. Special proteins seem to form a glassy shield around their cells, protecting delicate structures that would otherwise tear apart.

Popular articles sometimes call tardigrades indestructible, but biologists find that label dubious. Active tardigrades are fragile, and even tuns eventually perish if they are heated long enough. The animals are not invincible; they are simply experts at waiting until conditions improve.$q$, 'draft')
