-- Passage rg12-context-clues-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('aecd3af9-8e2c-5bb5-9588-8dfbb806fb29', 'rg12-context-clues-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Audition Piece$q$, $q$Adaeze had played the opening bars of the sonata so many times that her left hand moved through them on its own. The audition was in four days, and her teacher, Mr. Lindqvist, had begun treating every lesson like a rehearsal for disaster.

"Lovely," he said after she finished the first page. "If the panel happens to enjoy hearing a cello apologize for itself." His tone was sardonic, the dry, mocking humor of someone who had judged too many auditions to be impressed by anything, and Adaeze felt her face grow warm.

He was right, though, and she knew it. She played like a diffident guest at a stranger's dinner table, hesitant to take up space, softening every phrase that should have rung out. At school concerts no one noticed. In a room with three judges and a single microphone, every timid note would be exposed.

Her trepidation had grown all week. She had lain awake rehearsing the walk to the chair, imagining her bow skidding on the first note, picturing the judges trading glances. By Thursday even the sight of her cello case made her stomach tighten.

"Play it again," Mr. Lindqvist said, "and this time, if you miss a note, let it go. The great players are not the ones who never slip. They are the ones who keep their equanimity when they do, who stay calm enough that the listener never sees them flinch."

She began again. Halfway down the page her fourth finger landed flat, and the note soured. She kept going without a pause, and the next phrase came out broader and braver than any she had played that week.

When she lowered the bow, Mr. Lindqvist was quiet for a long moment. Then, in a rare magnanimous gesture, he admitted that his own audition at her age had been far worse, and that she was more ready than he had ever been.$q$, 'draft')
