-- Passage rg12-context-clues-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e28bc327-233d-5fe1-870a-4a6892115417', 'rg12-context-clues-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-context-clues'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Tuesdays at the Corner Table$q$, $q$Every Tuesday afternoon, Hana Kobayashi met her reading buddy at a corner table in the public library. Mateo was eight years old and, by his own cheerful admission, did not like books. During their first session he was recalcitrant, refusing to open the picture book she had chosen, sliding it to the edge of the table, and announcing that reading was for babies.

Hana tried to cajole him. She offered to read the first page herself, promised they could stop after ten minutes, and pointed out a funny drawing of a goat wearing boots. Mateo only crossed his arms tighter. Nothing she said could coax him into turning a single page.

By the third week Hana was exasperated. She had spent hours choosing books she thought he would love, and each one had been pushed aside. That week the program coordinator gently suggested pairing Mateo with a different tutor, but Hana insisted on staying. On the bus home she complained to her older brother, who listened patiently and then asked what Mateo actually talked about when he was not refusing to read.

"Garbage trucks," Hana said. "Constantly."

The following Tuesday she brought a thin, battered manual about city sanitation vehicles that she had found on a library cart. Mateo stared at the cover, pulled it toward him, and began sounding out the word hydraulic. Twenty minutes later he was still reading, pausing only to explain to her how a compactor worked.

His mood had changed completely. He was ebullient, bouncing in his chair, interrupting himself to point at diagrams, and talking so fast that the librarian glanced over twice. When his mother arrived, he insisted on checking the manual out.

Hana walked to the bus stop feeling vindicated. The decision she had defended to the coordinator, when even she had begun to doubt it, had turned out to be the right one.$q$, 'draft')
