-- Passage rg12-evidence-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('993a02db-dec6-55b6-b272-bccf9f74626c', 'rg12-evidence-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Retuning Velloran Hall$q$, $q$When Velloran Hall opened in 1968, critics praised its sweeping wooden ceiling and its 1,900 seats. Musicians were less pleased. Players on stage complained that they could not hear one another, and string sections often fell slightly out of time with the brass at the back. Later measurements confirmed that sound reflected from the high ceiling reached the stage too late to help performers stay together, while much of the hall's sound energy was absorbed by thick seat cushions and heavy curtains.

In 2021 the city's arts trust hired acoustician Rafael Osei to redesign the space without tearing it down. His team hung a set of curved panels above the stage, each one adjustable by motor, and replaced the curtains along the side walls with wooden surfaces angled to scatter sound. They also swapped the seat cushions for a thinner fabric chosen so that an empty seat absorbs about as much sound as a seated listener, which lets rehearsals in an empty hall sound closer to concerts.

Before the work, the hall's reverberation time, the number of seconds a loud sound takes to fade away, measured 1.4 seconds with an audience present. After the renovation it measured 2.0 seconds, within the range that many acousticians prefer for orchestral music. The resident orchestra's players, surveyed before and after the work, reported a sharp improvement in their ability to hear other sections.

The panels have a further use. For lectures and amplified concerts, which need a drier sound, technicians can lower the panels and unroll fabric banners stored in the ceiling. Osei has pointed out that this flexibility was part of the trust's financial reasoning, since the hall earns a large share of its income from events that are not orchestral concerts.$q$, 'draft')
