-- Passage rg12-evidence-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d041ab23-cde4-562a-ab06-5030fa931ce9', 'rg12-evidence-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$How Much Does the Wind Jump?$q$, $q$In outdoor sprinting and jumping events, a following wind can help an athlete in a way that has nothing to do with training. For that reason, many competitions place a gauge beside the runway or track and record the wind during each attempt. A result set with a tailwind above a fixed limit still counts for placing in that meet, but it cannot be entered as an official record.

Sports scientist Leilani Haverford wanted to know how much the wind actually matters for long jumpers at the regional level. She gathered results from 3,200 jumps across eleven seasons of the Dunmere Valley championships, each paired with its official wind reading. After adjusting for each jumper's personal best, she estimated that every meter per second of tailwind added about six centimeters to a typical jump. A headwind of the same speed cost slightly more, about seven centimeters.

Those numbers sound small, but at the regional level the gap between first and fourth place is often less than ten centimeters. In three of the eleven finals Haverford studied, the winner had jumped in a noticeably stronger tailwind than the runner-up. Haverford stresses that this does not mean those winners were undeserving, since wind shifts from moment to moment and every athlete faced the same changing conditions over the course of the event.

She does suggest one change. At Dunmere, the wind gauge has always stood near the take-off board, but the runway sits between two grandstands that channel gusts unevenly. A second gauge placed halfway down the runway recorded readings that differed from the first by as much as a meter per second on gusty days. Haverford argues that until meets measure wind more carefully, small differences in results should be interpreted with caution.$q$, 'draft')
