-- Passage rg12-evidence-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5d129fe6-6231-5d60-be2c-59bb5eb7dcc5', 'rg12-evidence-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-evidence-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Cooling Computers with a Cold Lake$q$, $q$Computers turn nearly all the electricity they use into heat, and a building packed with servers must remove that heat constantly or the machines will slow down and fail. Most computing centers do this with electric chillers, which work much like very large air conditioners. At Wendmere University, engineers decided to try a different approach: drawing cold water from the bottom of nearby Lake Orrin.

Lake Orrin is deep enough that its bottom layer stays at about four degrees Celsius all year, even when the surface warms in summer. The university laid a pipe more than a kilometer long to draw water from a depth of sixty meters. The cold lake water passes through a heat exchanger, where it cools a separate, closed loop of water that circulates through the computing center. The lake water never touches the equipment, and it is returned to the lake about nine degrees warmer than when it was drawn.

In its first full year, the system cut the electricity used for cooling by roughly 80 percent compared with the chillers it replaced. The chillers were kept in place as a backup and were switched on for only eleven days, during a repair to the intake pipe.

The project has drawn questions from people who fish and swim in the lake. Biologist Kwame Ashdown, whom the university hired to study the effects, found that the returned water spreads into a warm plume near the outflow, but that temperatures fall to normal within about two hundred meters. He has not found any change in fish numbers so far. However, he points out that his monitoring covers only three years, and that slow effects on the lake's layers of warm and cold water might take longer to appear. The university has agreed to fund his work for another decade.$q$, 'draft')
