  ('482b1439-a001-5f1f-99e3-9fb09ac04ca2', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null, 'medium',
   $q$Priya says the vase is "ruined," and Mr. Osei answers, "It's finished." Why does he choose "finished" instead of agreeing with "ruined"?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Finished\" means the vase is too damaged to keep, just as \"ruined\" does.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"\"Finished\" shows that he is annoyed the lesson took three whole weeks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"\"Finished\" means the vase needs to go back into the kiln to be repaired.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"\"Finished\" treats the vase as complete work to learn from, not a failure.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Finished" treats the vase as complete work to learn from, not a failure.$q$, $q$Both words say the vase can no longer change, but "ruined" judges it worthless while "finished" calls it complete, which fits his next line that a flaw "can teach you more." Treating the two words as meaning the same thing misses his correction.$q$, 'draft', '26d626ff-d221-5dbe-8f99-4fd15162e07d')
