  ('8192fa65-009a-5709-b593-b13f883e1cba', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The author calls a lichen "a pioneer that never sees the settlement it makes possible." What idea does this metaphor express?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Lichens prefer to live in towns and settlements rather than on rocks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-target"},{"text":"Lichens quickly crowd out the mosses and grasses that follow them.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Lichens start a slow process whose results arrive long after them.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Lichens were first discovered by early pioneers crossing open land.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Lichens start a slow process whose results arrive long after them.$q$, $q$Lichens make the "first thin layer of soil," and "over centuries" mosses, grasses and shrubs move in, so like pioneers they open ground for later life they do not live to see. The passage says the plants that follow move in, not that lichens crowd them out.$q$, 'draft', '0b4e9d1a-0e9b-5da2-b281-f8387da1d8bb')
