-- Passage rg12-figurative-language-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('82ef284f-3030-5958-9564-1bbb52a0a775', 'rg12-figurative-language-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$No One Is the Engine$q$, $q$At five-thirty in the morning, the river was so still that it looked as if someone had ironed it. Coach Adeyemi stood on the dock with a stopwatch and a thermos, and the eight rowers of the junior varsity boat carried their shell down to the water without a word.

Tomas Lindqvist sat in the seventh seat. He was the strongest rower on the team by every measure the coach kept, and he knew it. For weeks he had pulled harder than anyone, and for weeks the boat had felt sluggish, wobbling from side to side as if it could not decide which bank it preferred.

That morning, after the third practice piece, the coach motored alongside them in the launch. "Tomas," she called, "you are winning a race against your own crew."

The coxswain, a quick-talking junior named Rosalind Park, snorted, but Tomas felt his face go hot. "I'm giving it everything," he said.

"That's the problem," Coach Adeyemi said. "Give it less, at the same moment as everyone else. A boat goes fastest when no one is trying to be the engine."

On the next piece he did something that felt almost like cheating. He watched the back of the rower in front of him and matched her blade to the instant, entering the water when she did and finishing when she did. His legs burned less than before. And the boat, impossibly, began to run. It lifted and glided between strokes, the bow slicing the water with a soft hiss instead of a slap.

When they drifted to a stop, Rosalind checked the monitor and whistled. "Fastest we've gone all season," she said. "And you look like you barely broke a sweat."

Tomas laughed. He had spent a month trying to carry the boat, and the boat had only wanted him to let it carry him.$q$, 'draft')
