-- Passage rg12-figurative-language-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('823c1dda-3228-5a71-bc05-911744b562c3', 'rg12-figurative-language-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$What Your Photos Throw Away$q$, $q$Every time you snap a picture on a phone, a small act of deception takes place, and it is one you would almost certainly approve of. The raw information captured by the camera sensor is enormous, far too large to store thousands of images on a single device. So before the photo is saved, software quietly throws much of that information away.

This process is called lossy compression, and its cleverness lies in what it chooses to discard. Human vision is far more sensitive to changes in brightness than to fine changes in color, and we notice sharp edges more readily than subtle textures inside a smooth area. Compression software exploits these blind spots. It keeps the details our eyes guard most closely and trims the ones we would never miss, so a file can shrink to a tenth of its original size while looking nearly identical.

In a sense, a compressed photo is an accurate lie. It does not record what the scene actually contained; it records what a human viewer will believe the scene contained. The deception only shows when it is pushed too far. Squeeze an image too hard, or save it again and again, and blocky squares bloom across the sky while soft halos smear around every edge. Engineers call these flaws artifacts, a dry name for what is really the moment the magic trick is caught.

Not every field accepts the bargain. Medical scans and scientific measurements are often stored with lossless methods, which shrink files less but return every original value exactly. When a doctor is looking for a shadow a few pixels wide, the details that a casual viewer would never miss may be the only ones that matter.

The choice between the two approaches is ultimately a question of audience. A file built for human eyes can afford to forget; a file built for careful measurement cannot.$q$, 'draft')
