-- Passage rg12-figurative-language-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c0a43c5b-a0c3-5bed-9f55-c4629ce0a585', 'rg12-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why Museums Keep the Lights Low$q$, $q$Visitors to a museum's gallery of old drawings or textiles often notice something odd: the rooms are dim, sometimes so dim that it takes a minute for their eyes to adjust. It can feel as though the museum is hiding its own treasures. In fact, the low light is one of the most important things a museum does to protect them.

Light carries energy, and that energy slowly breaks apart the molecules in many pigments, dyes, and papers. Ultraviolet light, which we cannot even see, is especially destructive, but visible light causes damage as well. The harm is cumulative. A watercolor that spends a single afternoon in bright sunlight may show no change at all, yet the same exposure repeated for years can bleach its blues into ghostly gray and turn white paper the color of weak tea.

Conservators, the specialists who care for collections, often describe this as a budget. Each fragile object can absorb only so much light over its lifetime before it begins to fade, so every hour on display spends a little of an account that can never be refilled. To make the budget last, museums dim the lights, filter out ultraviolet rays, and rotate their most sensitive pieces, displaying a drawing for a few months and then resting it in a dark drawer for years.

This creates an uncomfortable paradox at the heart of the museum's mission. An object is preserved so that people can see it, but every viewing brings it closer to the day when there is nothing left to see. Conservators cannot escape this tension; they can only manage it with care.

So the next time a gallery seems gloomy, consider the dim lighting a kind of courtesy. The museum is not hiding its treasures from you. It is saving some of them for visitors who have not been born yet.$q$, 'draft')
