values
  ('3a1e11d7-f03a-5893-b8bb-76557c605a27', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-literary-structure-pov'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The final entry ends with the narrator concluding that the cloth "frightened away" the comet. Why is this an effective place for the author to end the story?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The mystery is solved for the reader but not for the narrator, so the ending completes the irony instead of correcting him.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The comet's disappearance is left unexplained, so the ending asks readers to decide whether the comet was real.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misses-irony"},{"text":"The narrator's sister admits she was wrong, so the ending shifts sympathy back to the young astronomer.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The narrator finally realizes his mistake, so the ending gives the story a neat lesson about cleaning equipment.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The mystery is solved for the reader but not for the narrator, so the ending completes the irony instead of correcting him.$q$, $q$Funmi's cloth gives readers the explanation, yet the narrator offers yet another absurd theory, so the gap in understanding peaks at the very end. He never realizes the truth, and the evidence leaves little real doubt.$q$, 'draft', '292d4398-3385-5e97-9236-fc0ae5fd6afc')
