-- Passage rg12-literary-structure-pov-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a240e601-b238-5151-b51a-ef6370d26827', 'rg12-literary-structure-pov-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Map That Was Never Sent$q$, $q$The map arrived at the archive in a cardboard tube with a note that said only, "Found in the attic. Thought someone should have it." Dr. Kalani Mahoe unrolled it on the long table and weighted the corners with books.

It was a survey of the river delta, drawn in brown ink in 1871. The coastline was finished, the soundings were finished, and the inland marshes were sketched with care. Only the northeast corner was blank, except for a pencil line that stopped halfway across the paper, as if the surveyor had been called away in the middle of a stroke.

One hundred and fifty years earlier, Eliza Brandt had stood in that northeast corner with her compass and her chain. She was the only woman on the survey crew, hired because her late father had trained her and because no one else would wade the marsh in October. She had been told the map would be sent to the capital and printed with her name in the corner.

She drew the coastline. She measured the depths. She worked until her boots split. Then a letter came from the capital: the project had been canceled, the funds moved elsewhere. She was to stop at once.

She stopped. She rolled up the map, carried it home, and set it in the attic, where it waited.

Dr. Mahoe studied the pencil line for a long time. Then she checked the modern charts and found that no one had ever properly surveyed that corner of the delta. The marsh had shifted, but the old survey markers would still be there, if someone knew where to look.

She reached for her own notebook and began to plan a trip.$q$, 'draft')
