  ('456beec6-1eae-50ba-b5d3-7b518f8f383f', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-literary-structure-pov'), null, 'hard',
   $q$The story ends as Dr. Mahoe "began to plan a trip," without showing whether she finishes the survey. How does this choice of ending contribute to the meaning?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It reveals that Dr. Mahoe is related to Eliza, so the ending explains why the map was sent to her.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"It turns Eliza's interrupted line into a task picked up again, so the ending stresses continuation over completion.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It shows that the corner can never be surveyed, so the ending confirms that Eliza's effort was wasted.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"It leaves the reader unsure whether the map is real, so the ending questions the whole flashback.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It turns Eliza's interrupted line into a task picked up again, so the ending stresses continuation over completion.$q$, $q$Ending with the start of a new plan echoes the pencil line that "stopped halfway," suggesting the work will continue across generations. The markers "would still be there," so the corner is not impossible to survey.$q$, 'draft', 'a240e601-b238-5151-b51a-ef6370d26827')
