-- Passage rg12-main-idea-summary-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('725e12f5-6347-5846-a7d8-5b61e97b87ea', 'rg12-main-idea-summary-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Water from the Morning Fog$q$, $q$Along the dry coast near the fictional village of Alto Seco, rain falls only a few days each year, yet thick fog rolls in from the ocean on most mornings. For generations, residents watched that moisture drift past their homes without any way to keep it. Water arrived by truck, and families stored it in barrels that had to last until the next delivery.

About fifteen years ago, a cooperative of farmers and an engineer named Teodora Quispe began testing a simple idea. They stretched fine plastic mesh between tall poles on a ridge facing the sea. As fog passes through the mesh, tiny droplets collide with the threads, merge into larger drops, and run down into a gutter. From there, pipes carry the water to a covered tank in the village.

The early results were modest. A single panel collected only a few buckets on a good day, and strong winds tore several nets during the first winter. Rather than abandon the project, the cooperative adjusted the design. They switched to a double layer of mesh, angled the panels toward the prevailing wind, and added cables so the frames could flex instead of snapping. Within three years, the ridge held forty panels that together supplied enough water for drinking and for a small greenhouse.

The nets have limits that the cooperative is careful to acknowledge. They work only where fog is frequent and the wind is steady, and the yield drops sharply in the clear months of late summer. Each panel also needs regular cleaning, because dust and salt clog the mesh. The village still pays for a truck delivery during the driest weeks.

Even so, the project changed daily life in Alto Seco. Families spend less on water, the greenhouse grows vegetables that once came from far inland, and young residents have learned to repair the panels. Visitors from other coastal towns now come to study the ridge, not because fog nets solve every water problem, but because the village showed how patient adjustment can turn a local resource into a dependable one.$q$, 'draft')
