-- Passage rg12-main-idea-summary-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1bd1fd44-46a0-568c-8fbe-05daf0e93544', 'rg12-main-idea-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$When Every Town Kept Its Own Time$q$, $q$Before the railways reached the fictional province of Merrow, each town set its clocks by the sun. Noon was the moment the sun stood highest over the town square, so a clock in the eastern port of Calder ran several minutes ahead of one in the western mill town of Fenwick. Nobody minded, because few people traveled far enough or fast enough to notice the difference.

The railway changed that. When the Merrow Line opened, a single train might stop in a dozen towns, each with its own version of the time. Printed timetables became nearly impossible to read, since a departure listed for nine o'clock meant something different at every station. Passengers missed connections, and on single tracks, where trains in opposite directions had to wait for each other, confusion about the time created real hazards.

In response, the railway company adopted one standard time for its whole network, based on the clock at its headquarters in Calder. Station clocks displayed railway time, and conductors carried watches set to it. For a while, many towns kept two clocks side by side, one for the railway and one for the sun, and residents had to think about which one an appointment meant.

The change met resistance. Some people argued that time belonged to the sun and to each community, not to a business. A newspaper in Fenwick complained that its readers were being told to eat lunch by a clock in another city. Yet the convenience of a shared time slowly won out. Shops, schools and courts began to follow railway time, and two decades after the line opened, the provincial assembly made it the legal time for all of Merrow.

The episode shows how a new technology can quietly reshape habits that once seemed natural. Local solar time had made sense when communities lived mostly apart. Once fast travel tied those communities together, they needed a common measure, and the clock on the wall came to reflect the network rather than the sky.$q$, 'draft')
