-- Passage rg12-main-idea-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ee2e4caa-0d30-50c9-82b9-b7a35382e5a5', 'rg12-main-idea-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Cooling a City from the Rooftops$q$, $q$On a summer afternoon, a dark rooftop can become far hotter than the air around it. Asphalt shingles and tar absorb most of the sunlight that strikes them and release that energy as heat, both into the building below and into the surrounding streets. Multiply that effect across thousands of roofs, and an entire city can stay several degrees warmer than nearby farmland, especially at night.

In the fictional city of Corvana, planners decided to test whether lighter roofs could reduce that extra heat. With funding from the city, crews applied a pale reflective coating to three hundred roofs in two neighborhoods, including apartment buildings, a library and several small stores. Researchers placed sensors on those roofs and on similar dark roofs nearby, then compared readings over two summers.

The results were striking. On the hottest days, coated roofs stayed much cooler at the surface than uncoated ones, and the top floors beneath them were noticeably more comfortable. Residents of coated apartment buildings used their air conditioners less, which lowered their electric bills. Street temperatures in the test neighborhoods dropped slightly, though the change was smaller than the change on the roofs themselves.

The study also revealed trade-offs. In winter, the coated buildings lost some of the free warmth that dark roofs would have absorbed, so heating costs rose a little. The coatings gathered dirt and needed washing or reapplication every few years to stay reflective. A few neighbors in taller buildings complained about glare from the bright surfaces below their windows.

The planners concluded that reflective roofs are a useful tool but not a complete answer. Because the benefit to streets was modest, they recommended pairing coatings with shade trees and with pale paving in parking lots. Corvana now offers small grants for roof coatings while expanding its tree planting program, treating the rooftop project as one part of a wider effort to keep the city livable as summers grow hotter.$q$, 'draft')
