-- Passage rg12-main-idea-summary-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b7112449-849e-5ec4-9e62-f4682a0e6017', 'rg12-main-idea-summary-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Hearing Old Voices with Light$q$, $q$More than a century ago, before flat records became common, many sounds were captured on wax cylinders. A needle traced vibrations into a spiral groove on the surface of a small tube, and a second needle could later play them back. Families, traveling researchers and local musicians used cylinders to record songs, speeches and stories that were never written down.

Today, many of those cylinders sit in archives such as the fictional Halvorsen Sound Archive, and they are in trouble. Wax grows brittle with age, and many cylinders are cracked, warped or covered with mold. Playing one with a needle, the only method once available, can scrape away part of the groove and damage it further. Some cylinders are so fragile that they cannot be played at all.

A newer method avoids touching the cylinder. Engineers at the archive place a cylinder on a slowly turning stand and scan its surface with a focused beam of light. A camera measures the exact depth and shape of the groove at thousands of points, building a detailed three-dimensional map. Software then calculates how a needle would have moved through that groove and turns the movement into sound.

The results can be remarkable. The archive has recovered recordings from cylinders broken into several pieces by scanning each piece and joining the maps digitally. Software can also skip over cracks that would have made a needle jump. Among the first recordings restored were work songs from a fishing village that no longer exists, sung by people whose names survive only on handwritten labels.

The method is slow and costly, and the archive can scan only a few cylinders each week. The recovered sound is also limited by the original recording, which was often faint and scratchy. Still, the archivists argue that the effort is worth it. Each scan preserves a recording before the wax decays further, and it does so without causing the very damage that playback once did.$q$, 'draft')
