-- Passage rg12-story-details-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('75f00982-6ea9-5f47-8a5f-aec810ba2713', 'rg12-story-details-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Sheet 14$q$, $q$The county survey office kept its oldest maps in flat steel drawers that squealed when opened, and Teodora Vasquez had learned to open them slowly. Her summer job was simple on paper: compare each hand-drawn map from the 1890s with a modern aerial photograph and mark every difference in red pencil.

Most differences were small. A road had been straightened, a barn had disappeared, a pond had become a parking lot. Then she reached Sheet 14, and the Marrow River was in the wrong place.

It was not off by a little. On the old map the river curved east of the Pell farm in a wide loop, while in the photograph it ran a quarter mile farther west, nearly straight. Teodora checked the scale twice, then a third time, before she carried both sheets to Mr. Adeyemi's desk.

He looked for a long time without speaking. "Either the surveyor made a mistake," he said at last, "or the river did something."

"Rivers don't move a quarter mile."

"Not in a summer," he agreed. He opened a drawer of his own and took out a folder of letters, the paper brown at the edges. One, from a farmer named Pell, complained in careful handwriting that the spring flood of 1903 had "taken the east field and given it to my neighbor." Mr. Adeyemi tapped the line with one finger and said nothing more.

Teodora read the letter twice. Then she went back to her table, and instead of marking Sheet 14 in red pencil as an error, she wrote a small note in the margin: See Pell letter, 1903. Possible channel change. Not confirmed.

At five o'clock Mr. Adeyemi passed her table on his way out. He glanced at the margin, raised his eyebrows slightly, and kept walking. The next morning she found a second folder on her chair, labeled Flood Records 1900 to 1910, with no note attached.$q$, 'draft')
