-- Passage rg12-story-details-inference-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('863dcbbe-f409-542b-80cc-cc7ac437f75f', 'rg12-story-details-inference-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Readings$q$, $q$My grandmother kept the weather for forty years.
Each morning at seven she walked to the white box by the fence,
read the thermometer, emptied the rain gauge,
and wrote three numbers in a ledger with a green cover.
She never missed a day that I know of.
When she moved to the apartment on Larch Street,
she left the ledger on the kitchen table
with a pencil laid across it, sharpened at both ends.
She did not say a word about it.

So now I am the one who goes out at seven.
The first week I wrote the numbers late,
sometimes at noon, guessing at the morning.
Then I found her old entries for the same weeks,
forty Junes stacked like plates,
and I saw how she wrote zero rain as 0.00,
never a blank, never a shrug, as if nothing
were also something worth measuring.
After that I set an alarm.

On Sundays I call her and read the week aloud.
She listens without interrupting,
and when I finish she asks only one thing:
whether the wind came from the west.
I tell her what the vane says.
I do not know why she asks, or what she hears in it,
but she says thank you as if I had brought her a gift.$q$, 'draft')
