   $q$Which statement best reflects what the poem says directly and what it leaves uncertain about the grandmother's Sunday question?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The poem states that she asks about the west wind, but the speaker admits not knowing why she asks.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The poem explains that a west wind reminds her of the house, so the question is a form of homesickness.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"The poem states that she asks only to be polite and does not care about the answer.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The poem shows the question is a test of whether the speaker is really taking readings.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstated-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The poem states that she asks about the west wind, but the speaker admits not knowing why she asks.$q$, $q$The speaker says plainly, "I do not know why she asks, or what she hears in it," so the reason is left open. Homesickness is a possible reading, but the poem never explains the question, so claiming it does misstates the text.$q$, 'draft', '863dcbbe-f409-542b-80cc-cc7ac437f75f')
