-- Passage rg12-text-structure-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('6d2966de-5d75-591e-b693-3086bce93110', 'rg12-text-structure-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Reading the Mud of Lake Orvik$q$, $q$Every winter, when ice seals the surface of Lake Orvik, the water beneath it grows almost perfectly still. Nothing stirs the bottom, and the finest particles of clay drift down through the dark until they settle in a thin, dark film. In spring the ice breaks, meltwater rushes in from the surrounding hills, and coarser, paler silt rains onto the lakebed. One pale band and one dark band together record a single year. Geologists call such a pair a varve, and Lake Orvik holds more than eleven thousand of them.

Counting those layers sounds simple, but it requires patience and some ingenuity. In 2019 a team led by sediment geologist Ingrid Saelen drove a hollow steel tube into the lakebed from a raft and pulled up a core nine meters long. Back in the laboratory, the team froze the core, sliced it lengthwise, and photographed it under strong light. Graduate student Tomasz Wrobel then counted the bands by eye, while a computer program counted them independently from the images.

The two counts did not match at first. In several sections, unusually wet springs had laid down two pale bands in a single year, which made the program count extra years. Wrobel caught these doubles because the false bands lacked the dark winter film that should sit above each true spring layer. Once the team corrected for them, the human and machine counts agreed to within forty years across the whole core.

Why go to such trouble? Because the varves are more than a calendar. Thick spring layers mark years of heavy snowmelt, and thin ones mark dry years. Grains of pollen trapped in each band reveal which trees grew on the hills at the time. Read together, the layers form a year-by-year record of the region's climate stretching back to the end of the last ice age, far longer than any written weather record.

The record has limits. A landslide or a powerful storm can scramble a section of layers, and no single core captures every corner of a lake. Still, Saelen's team now plans to drill two more cores elsewhere in the lake, so that each record can check the others.$q$, 'draft')
