-- Passage rg12-theme-summary-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('03aa796a-0ab7-593c-a290-c6cc033d038e', 'rg12-theme-summary-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Aisle Nine$q$, $q$My father points at the bin of hinges
and asks me, in our language, which one
will hold a door that swings both ways.
I ask the clerk in English,
and the clerk answers me, not him,
as if the question had been mine.

I have been doing this since I was eight.
The landlord's letters, the dentist's forms,
the phone call about the water bill:
I learned the word "overdue"
before I learned to spell it.

At eight it felt like a magic trick.
At seventeen it feels like a coat
that fit me once
and still gets handed to me every morning.

Today the clerk talks fast about load ratings and screws.
I translate the words and lose the jokes.
My father laughs anyway, at the right moment,
because he has read the clerk's face
the way he reads a stubborn engine,
by listening past the noise.

In the parking lot he holds up the hinge
and says, in careful English, "Both ways."
He is proud of the phrase. I am proud of him.
And somewhere under that I feel a little lost,
the way you might feel when a bridge you built
starts carrying traffic you did not send.

Tonight I will fill out his license renewal,
and he will check my spelling of our street.
Neither of us will say
which one of us is helping.$q$, 'draft')
