-- Passage rg12-theme-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('94c40bda-aadc-5df1-9bef-f79653aa3c85', 'rg12-theme-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Purple, With a Star$q$, $q$Ji-ho Shin had spent most of his junior year designing a printed prosthetic hand for a volunteer group that made free devices for children. His version used a new tendon layout that gripped eleven percent more firmly than the group's standard model, and he had tested it on a scale until the numbers held steady. When the group matched him with an eight-year-old named Amara Bello, who had been born without fingers on her left hand, he printed the hand in a sturdy gray plastic and brought it to her family's apartment, certain of its quality.

Amara looked at it politely, the way children look at a sweater from a distant relative. She tried it on, picked up a cup, set it down, and took the hand off again. Her mother explained, apologetically, that Amara preferred her old hand, even though it was weaker, because it was light enough to wear all day.

Ji-ho asked Amara what she would change. She considered the question with great seriousness. She wanted it lighter. She wanted it purple. She wanted a flat spot on the back for stickers, because the other kids at school asked about her hand, and she would rather they asked about the stickers.

On the bus home Ji-ho almost decided that her requests were trivial compared with grip strength. Then he remembered that she had taken the hand off. The strongest grip in the world did nothing in a drawer.

He spent three weekends on the redesign. He thinned the palm, which cost him some of the extra grip, and printed it in a violet so bright his sister complained about it. He added a smooth panel on the back. When he brought the new version over, Amara put it on at once, and she was still wearing it when he left two hours later, a cartoon star already stuck in the center of the panel.

That spring Ji-ho rewrote the first page of the group's design guide. The old first step read, "Maximize mechanical performance." The new one read, "Ask the person who will wear it."$q$, 'draft')
