-- Passage rg12-theme-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('14de3638-bd5a-502a-a9c5-697b5d2d8f23', 'rg12-theme-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Marmot Who Kept the Map$q$, $q$High on a mountain meadow lived a marmot named Teska who knew more about the ground than any creature for miles. Over many summers she had learned which burrows flooded in spring, which stayed dry, and which had back tunnels a fox could not follow. She kept all of it in her head, and she kept her head to herself.

When young marmots came to ask where to dig, Teska answered with riddles. "Dig where the snow leaves last," she would say, or, "Dig where the wind cannot find you," and then she would watch them guess. Some guessed well. Many did not, and those came back to her each spring, wet and shivering, to ask again. Teska liked being asked. Each question reminded the meadow that it needed her.

One autumn a rockslide changed the shape of the eastern slope, and Teska, who was growing old, could no longer climb it to learn what had changed. The young marmots climbed it easily. They found new hollows and dry ledges and argued about them among themselves, but none of them had Teska's years of knowledge to judge which places would hold through a winter. Teska, for her part, had no way to see the places they described.

For the first time, she had to ask them. Slowly, and with some embarrassment, she began to explain everything she knew, plainly and without riddles: why a spot that looked dry in autumn might flood in spring, and how to read the moss on a stone. The young ones listened, then climbed, then came back and told her what they had found. Together they drew a map in the dust beneath the great boulder, and they redrew it each season.

Teska was asked fewer questions after that. She found, to her surprise, that she was visited more.$q$, 'draft')
